        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "47e0faf7-ca82-56cb-8410-5f3e8d1c0671",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lincoln Steffens, Monique Wittig, Doc Ames\n- Works: McClure's, \"Tweed Days in St. Louis\", \"The Shame of the Cities\"\n- Topics: organized crime in Minneapolis, feminism, gender roles\n\nFacts:\n- Lincoln Steffens was born on April 6, 1866, and later became a New York reporter.\n- In 1903, organized crime in Minneapolis first attracted national attention when Doc Ames was exposed by Lincoln Steffens in the book \"The Shame of the Cities\".\n- Lincoln Steffens launched a series of articles in McClure's called \"Tweed Days in St. Louis\", and those articles were later published together as the book \"The Shame of the Cities\".\n- Monique Wittig was born on July 13, 1935, and was a French author and feminist theorist.\n- Monique Wittig wrote about overcoming socially enforced gender roles and coined the phrase \"heterosexual contract\".\n- Monique Wittig died on January 3, 2003.\n- Lincoln Steffens died on August 9, 1936.\n- Doc Ames lived from 1842 to 1911 and was a thug and mayor.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "67c87a74-5a0c-5bd3-b630-99f28f617114",
        "text": "Page: Lincoln Steffens. Sentence: Lincoln Joseph Steffens (April 6, 1866 – August 9, 1936) was a New York reporter who launched a series of articles in \"McClure's\", called \"Tweed Days in St. Louis\", that would later be published together in a book titled \"The Shame of the Cities\".\nPage: Monique Wittig. Sentence: Monique Wittig (] ; July 13, 1935 – January 3, 2003) was a French author and feminist theorist who wrote about overcoming socially enforced gender roles and who coined the phrase \"heterosexual contract\".\nPage: Organized crime in Minneapolis. Sentence: Organized crime in Minneapolis first attracted national attention in 1903, when thug and mayor Doc Ames (1842-1911) was exposed by Lincoln Steffens in the book \"The Shame of the Cities\".",
